assage 1 again: \n\"In Germany, Walther Bothe and his student Herbert Becker had used polonium to bombard beryllium with alpha particles, producing an unusual form of radiation. Chadwick had his Australian 1851 Exhibition scholar, Hugh Webster, duplicate their results. To Chadwick, this was evidence of something that he and Rutherford had been hypothesising for years: the neutron...\" \n\nSince James Chadwick is overwhelmingly the most detailed participant in major projects (MAUD/Manhattan) mentioned alongside the discovery, and his work *was* the culmination of finding evidence, I will pivot to searching if any other person who provided critical early data was also involved later. But based on the text, Chadwick is the one whose subsequent participation is heavily documented.\n\nThe structure suggests Person X -> Evidence $\rightarrow$ Participation in Y. Since only Chadwick has extensive documentation for both his discovery AND deep participation in major government/military projects (MAUD, Manhattan), I must conclude that either: 1) The question intends to identify Chadwick despite the slightly awkward phrasing ('provided evidence' might be interpreted broadly as contributing scientific foundations leading to his own work), or 2) no single person fits both criteria perfectly based on the text.\n\nIf the intent is 'Who was central to the neutron discovery and later participated in major state projects?', the answer is James Chadwick. I will proceed with this interpretation, as it's the most informationally supported path through the document set.", "finding": "James Chadwick discovered the neutron (1932) and was a key participant in subsequent major endeavors like writing the MAUD Report and working on the Manhattan Project.\nEvidence for the neutron included: Walther Bothe and his student Herbert Becker producing unusual radiation by bombarding beryllium with polonium; Frédéric and Irène Joliot-Curie ejecting protons from paraffin wax when this unknown radiation 